# THE DEEP, SLAB 2 — 46 rooms, and the king at the end of them instead of the
# bottom of a stair (rome, 2026-09-26: the deep was a straight shot to the boss;
# double it, and give the king rooms of his own).
#
# Slab 1 is the original deep, laid down in migrations 021/036/069 before this
# pipeline existed; it has no source file and this one does not rewrite it. Its
# rooms appear here only as !existing stubs, to hang the new doors on.
#
# THE RULE THE NEW GROUND KEEPS: on every floor the way down is at the far end
# from where you came in. Migration 290 cut the old line (nave -> silted stair ->
# processional -> threshold -> throne); these rooms are what you cross instead.
#
#   FLOOR 1, THE FLOODED HALLS. The garrison under the dungeon, drowned. The west
#     wing past the Weeping Cells ends in the charnel chute (down). The east way
#     is the roots of the Root-Choked Vault. Three rooms of water-works over the
#     canal and the weir.
#   FLOOR 2, THE DARK. Dry, blind country. The chute lands on its west side and
#     has to be crossed to the Worm Cloister; the roots land at the Sump.
#   FLOOR 3, THE BONE CHOIR. Where the marrow-cantors sing — and where the crown
#     is kept (mig 243: the choir keeps it, not the king). Entered from the Marrow
#     Road and from the Silted Stair (292), and it is what opens on the
#     Processional (292). A back way runs up its drain to the death cell.
#   FLOOR 4, THE KING'S COURT. Oratory, reliquary, death cell and court, now a
#     ring around the king's rooms that never opens onto them.
#   THE KING'S ROOMS. Black Threshold -> the King's Stair -> the Waiting Hall ->
#     the Sunken Throne -> the Hoard. One door in.
#   EACH KING'S OWN ROOM. One king spawn, and the bloodline roll picks the room
#     (VARIANT_HOMES): the Forgotten King on the Sunken Throne, the Marrow-King on
#     the Marrow Seat past the Bone Font, the Drowned God in the God's Pool below
#     the Still Cradle.
#   THE TIDEWAYS. Four more rooms of siege-works off the Salt-Vault, looping back
#     to the Breathing-Hall; and the Cold Hearth's chimney drops to the
#     Silt-Chapel (292), so the tide's long way reaches the court as well.
#
# THE SQUARES. Up/down are map diagonals (down = +1,+1). Every room below sits on
# a square its own doors agree with; the two seams that cannot (the Silted Stair
# and the Processional, both boxed in by slab 1) are written by hand in 292 so
# the builder does not seed the Choir off them and drag it across the sheet.
#
#   y47   cold-pantry(-4) kitchens(-3)            lock-keeper(3) sluice-gear(4) flood-mark(5)
#   y48   chute(-4) silt-bank(-3) sunk-bell(-2) flooded-cloister(-1)
#   y49   chute-foot(-3) chain-well(-2) sluice-walk(-1) [weeping-cells 0]
#   y50   scraped-hall(-4) blind-gallery(-3)
#   y51   wormcast(-4) feeling-wall(-3) gnawed-arch(-2) bored-passage(-1) drag(0)   cooperage(7)
#   y52   crawlway(-1) root-shaft(2)                                               cable-walk(7) brine(8)
#   y53   bone-sift(0) embalming(3) kings-stair(5)                                 lung-vent(8)
#   y54   scavengers-shelf(0) choir-stair(3) bone-vestry(4) waiting-hall(6)
#   y55   lectern(2) singing-gallery(3) cantors-cells(4) drain-run(5) [throne 6]
#   y56   bone-organ(2) choir-floor(3) held-note(4) cantors-door(5) choir-drain(6) [hoard 7]
#   y57   silt-fall(2) bone-font(3) long-pews(4)
#   y58   marrow-seat(3)          (the Marrow-King's)
#   off the Tideways: gods-pool(10,56), below the Still Cradle (the Drowned God's)

!region deep

# ---- FLOOR 1: THE FLOODED HALLS — the west wing ------------------------------

## weeping-cells | The Weeping Cells
!existing
> west   the-sluice-walk

## the-sluice-walk | The Sluice-Walk
A stone ledge a boot wide runs along a channel of black water, the wall at your
other shoulder slick and close. Iron rings are set into it every few paces at the
height of a hand, and every one of them is worn bright.
> east   weeping-cells
> west   the-chain-well
> north  the-flooded-cloister

## the-chain-well | The Chain-Well
A square shaft of water in the floor, and hanging down into it from somewhere
above, a chain with links the size of a fist. It goes down further than the water
lets you see. Every so often it moves, one link's worth, and is still again.
> east   the-sluice-walk
> north  the-sunk-bell

## the-flooded-cloister | The Flooded Cloister
Four arcades around a court that is a pool now, the water standing level with the
walk so that you cannot tell where the path stops. The pillars go down into their
reflections, and the reflections go on further than the pillars do.
> south  the-sluice-walk
> west   the-sunk-bell

## the-sunk-bell | The Sunk Bell
A bell the height of a man lies on its lip in the middle of the floor, cracked
from crown to rim and half sunk in silt. It fell from somewhere. There is nothing
above it now but dark. When the water moves against it, it hums.
> east   the-flooded-cloister
> south  the-chain-well
> west   the-silt-bank

## the-silt-bank | The Silt-Bank
The flood has dropped its load here: a long bank of grey silt shelving up out of
the water, soft and faintly warm, printed all over with tracks. Most of the tracks
come up out of the water. Fewer of them go back in.
> east   the-sunk-bell
> north  the-drowned-kitchens
> west   the-charnel-chute

## the-drowned-kitchens | The Drowned Kitchens
Hearths big enough to stand in, a spit-rack rusted into one red lump, and stone
troughs where a garrison's food was once washed. The water is at the knee and very
cold. Something has been keeping the troughs clear.
> south  the-silt-bank
> west   the-cold-pantry

## the-cold-pantry | The Cold Pantry
A low store-room on a step above the flood, lined with shelves cut into the rock.
Whatever was kept here was kept against a long siege, and the siege won. The
shelves are bare except for the bones, which somebody has sorted by size.
> east   the-drowned-kitchens
> south  the-charnel-chute

## the-charnel-chute | The Charnel Chute
A slot in the floor, polished like a slide by everything that ever went down it:
the garrison's dead, when there got to be too many to carry up. The draught that
comes out of it is dry and smells of dust and old marrow. There is no stair. There
is only down.
> north  the-cold-pantry
> east   the-silt-bank
> down   the-chute-foot

# ---- FLOOR 1: over the canal and the weir -----------------------------------

## black-canal | The Black Canal
!existing
> north  the-lock-keepers-room

## the-weir | The Broken Weir
!existing
> north  the-sluice-gear

## the-lock-keepers-room | The Lock-Keeper's Room
A little room above the canal where somebody once worked the water: a stool, a
peg-board of tallies for barges that never came, and a window of iron bars looking
down on the black channel. The tallies stop partway along a row.
> south  black-canal
> east   the-sluice-gear

## the-sluice-gear | The Sluice-Gear
The winding gear for the weir's gates: two great wooden drums on iron axles, their
chains running down through slots in the floor. The drums are swollen and split and
will never turn again. Under the slots the weir roars on, as if nobody had ever
tried to stop it.
> south  the-weir
> west   the-lock-keepers-room
> east   the-flood-mark

## the-flood-mark | The Flood-Mark
A narrow room whose walls are ruled with high-water marks, each one cut by hand and
dated in some old reckoning, climbing the stone year by year. The last and highest
is above your head. Nothing was cut after it.
> west   the-sluice-gear

# ---- FLOOR 2: THE DARK -------------------------------------------------------

## the-chute-foot | The Chute's Foot
The chute spits you out onto a slope of loose bone that shifts and settles under
you like shingle. Everything that was ever sent down lies here in a fan, the newest
on top. Getting back up means climbing the chute against its own polish.
> up     the-charnel-chute
> south  the-blind-gallery

## the-blind-gallery | The Blind Gallery
A gallery built for eyes that has lost the use of them. Niches are cut all along
the walls at head height, each with a stone lamp in it, and every lamp is full of
dust. Nobody has lit anything here in longer than the dust has been falling.
> north  the-chute-foot
> west   the-scraped-hall
> south  the-feeling-wall

## the-scraped-hall | The Scraped Hall
Every surface in this room has been scraped. Not carved: scraped, in long parallel
grooves at the height of something crouching, as if it went round and round the
walls feeling for the way out. It did not find it here.
> east   the-blind-gallery
> south  the-wormcast-pit

## the-wormcast-pit | The Wormcast Pit
A round pit in the floor, filled to the brim with coiled grey castings, cold and
dry and packed as hard as mortar. Some of the coils are as thick as your thigh. The
newest ones, on top, are not dry.
> north  the-scraped-hall
> east   the-feeling-wall

## the-feeling-wall | The Feeling Wall
A passage so narrow and so dark that the stone at your shoulder has been worn
smooth by hands. Not yours, not recent, and not all of them hands. The polish runs
at the height of a hip, and then, lower down, at the height of a belly.
> north  the-blind-gallery
> west   the-wormcast-pit
> east   the-gnawed-arch

## the-gnawed-arch | The Gnawed Arch
An arch of good dressed stone with half its blocks gnawed away, the tooth-marks on
them the width of a thumb. It has held this long on the other half. The bitten
stone lies in a heap underneath, and none of it has been eaten. Only taken down.
> west   the-feeling-wall
> east   the-bored-passage

## worm-cloister | The Worm Cloister
!existing
> south  the-bored-passage

## the-bored-passage | The Bored Passage
The worm-holes of the cloister open here into one bore big enough to walk in bent
double, perfectly round and perfectly smooth, its floor grooved down the middle by
the weight of whatever made it. The groove is not old.
> north  worm-cloister
> west   the-gnawed-arch
> south  the-crawlway

## carrion-gallery | The Carrion Gallery
!existing
> west   the-crawlway
> south  the-bone-sift

## the-crawlway | The Crawlway
A low run between the bored passage and the carrion gallery, too low to stand in,
floored with bone-grit ground fine. You can smell the gallery long before you see
it, and the air is warm in the way a den is warm.
> east   carrion-gallery
> north  the-bored-passage

## the-undertow | The Undertow
!existing
> west   the-drag

## the-lightless-march | The Lightless March
!existing
> south  the-drag

## the-drag | The Drag
The floor slopes toward the undertow, and something has been dragged down it so
often that it has worn a channel in the stone, shallow and smooth and the width of
a body. It runs one way. Nothing has been dragged back up.
> east   the-undertow
> north  the-lightless-march

## the-gasping-dark | The Gasping Dark
!existing
> west   the-bone-sift

## the-bone-sift | The Bone-Sift
The carrion gallery's midden drains down into this low room through a grate, and
the fall sorts what comes through: the fine stuff in drifts along the walls, the
heavy stuff in the middle, and teeth everywhere, like gravel. The air is dry and
smells of old broth.
> north  carrion-gallery
> east   the-gasping-dark
> south  the-scavengers-shelf

## the-scavengers-shelf | The Scavengers' Shelf
A shelf of rock above the sift, reached by a scramble, where something has been
bringing the best of the fall to eat in peace. The floor is a litter of cracked
long-bones, every one split lengthways and licked clean.
> north  the-bone-sift

## the-sump | The Sump
!existing
> east   the-root-shaft

## root-vault | The Root-Choked Vault
!existing
> down   the-root-shaft

## the-root-shaft | The Root-Shaft
The roots that split the vault above go on down through its floor, and here they
hang in a shaft like the ropes of a bell-tower, thick and grey and long dead. They
will take a climber's weight. They are the only reason anybody comes this way.
> west   the-sump
> up     root-vault

# ---- FLOOR 3: THE BONE CHOIR -------------------------------------------------

## the-marrow-road | The Marrow Road
!existing
> down   the-choir-stair

## the-choir-stair | The Choir Stair
The marrow road does not end here; it goes down, a stair of knucklebones set on
edge like cobbles, each step worn to a dish in the middle. From below comes a sound,
faint and steady, that you will spend the next few rooms trying to believe is wind.
> up     the-marrow-road
> south  the-singing-gallery

## the-singing-gallery | The Singing Gallery
A long gallery walled in skulls laid jaw-out, thousands of them, and every jaw
propped open with a sliver of bone. When the draught moves through the room it
moves through all of them at once. The note is low and it does not stop. It is not
wind.
> north  the-choir-stair
> west   the-lectern-room
> south  the-choir-floor

## the-lectern-room | The Lectern Room
A bronze lectern green with age stands in the middle of the floor, holding a book
whose pages are cut from something thinner than vellum. The marks on them are not
writing: they are rows of lengths and depths, how long and how low. A hymnal, for
voices that no longer need breath.
> east   the-singing-gallery
> south  the-bone-organ

## the-bone-organ | The Bone Organ
One wall of this room is an instrument: long-bones of every length, bored and
fitted rank on rank into a frame of ribs, the longest thicker than a man. The
bellows rotted away long ago. Something still presses its keys now and then, and
the room answers with a note you feel in your teeth.
> north  the-lectern-room
> south  the-silt-fall

## the-silt-fall | The Silt-Fall
The silted stair spills out into this room through a gap in the ceiling, a long
frozen slump of black silt stepped where feet have gone up and down it. Beneath it
the bones of the floor are furred grey. The choir's hum comes through the walls
muffled here, like a song heard under water.
> north  the-bone-organ
> east   the-bone-font

## the-choir-floor | The Choir Floor
The heart of the choir: two banks of stone stalls facing each other across a floor
of polished bone, empty and ranked and waiting. Every seat is worn to the shape of
whoever sat in it. The air hums very slightly, with something that stopped a moment
before you came in.
> north  the-singing-gallery
> south  the-bone-font

## the-bone-font | The Bone Font
A font carved from a single great skull, too big to have belonged to anything you
would want to meet, set on a pedestal of stacked vertebrae. It is full to the brim
with water that is perfectly clear and perfectly still. Nothing has drunk from it.
Nothing has dared.
> west   the-silt-fall
> north  the-choir-floor
> east   the-long-pews
> south  the-marrow-seat

## the-long-pews | The Long Pews
Row on row of bone benches run off into the dark, every one facing the same blank
wall. The congregation is still here, after a fashion: a finger-bone on this seat, a
jaw on that one, set down carefully, as if keeping a place for someone who stepped
out.
> west   the-bone-font
> north  the-held-note

## the-held-note | The Held Note
A round chamber shaped so that a sound made in it never ends. Your step comes back
to you, and back again, and is still coming back when you take the next. Stand
still and the room goes on with whatever was last sung in it, very faint, the same
note, for ever.
> south  the-long-pews
> north  the-cantors-cells
> east   the-cantors-door

## the-cantors-cells | The Cantors' Cells
A row of cells too small to lie down in, each with a stone seat facing the wall and
a hollow worn into the wall at the height of a mouth. They practised here, alone,
face to the stone, for a very long time. Some of the hollows go right through.
> south  the-held-note

## the-cantors-door | The Cantors' Door
A narrow door of black stone at the foot of the choir's last stair, and the moment
you are through it the hum stops dead, the way sound stops at the edge of a church.
Below, the skull-walls of the processional begin. The cantors pass this way often.
They never linger on the far side.
> west   the-held-note
> east   the-choir-drain

## the-choir-drain | The Choir Drain
The death cell's drain comes out here through a grate in the ceiling, and under it
a stain runs down the wall and across the floor into a gutter. The cantors have
built a low wall of skulls along the gutter, as if to keep whatever runs in it out
of the choir.
> west   the-cantors-door
> up     the-drain-run

# ---- FLOOR 4: THE KING'S COURT -----------------------------------------------

## kings-oratory | The King's Oratory
!existing
> south  the-embalming-room

## bone-reliquary | The Bone Reliquary
!existing
> west   the-embalming-room
> south  the-bone-vestry

## the-death-cell | The Death Cell
!existing
> west   the-bone-vestry
> south  the-drain-run

## the-embalming-room | The Embalming Room
Stone slabs with channels cut along their edges, jars stoppered with wax, hooks,
and fine blades gone black. The King's household was made ready here before being
set out in the court to wait on him for ever. Some of the jars are still full. The
wax on those is new.
> north  kings-oratory
> east   bone-reliquary

## the-bone-vestry | The Bone-Vestry
The court's robing room: pegs along the walls, and on the pegs, vestments stiff with
age and sewn all over with small bones in patterns like scales. The cantors come up
from the choir to dress here. One peg is bare, and the dust beneath it is not.
> north  bone-reliquary
> east   the-death-cell

## the-drain-run | The Drain-Run
The death cell's floor slopes to a drain, and this is where the drain goes: a low
culvert running down and away under the court, its floor dark and slick. Old iron
grates are set across it at intervals, and every one is bent outward, from the
inside.
> north  the-death-cell
> down   the-choir-drain

# ---- THE KING'S ROOMS --------------------------------------------------------

## black-threshold | The Black Threshold
!existing
> down   the-kings-stair

## the-kings-stair | The King's Stair
Past the threshold the cold goes down a broad stair cut for processions, every step
wide enough for six abreast and every one of them empty. The walls are bare stone
and unmarked: whatever waits at the bottom never needed to be announced. With each
step down, the water sounds nearer.
> up     black-threshold
> down   the-waiting-hall

## sunken-throne | The Sunken Throne
!existing
> north  the-waiting-hall

## the-waiting-hall | The Waiting Hall
An antechamber of black stone where petitioners once stood to wait on the King's
pleasure. They are still standing: rows of figures gone to salt and silt, waist-deep
in still water, every face turned toward the far door. None of them was ever
called. The door in front of them stands open.
> up     the-kings-stair
> south  sunken-throne

# ---- THE TIDEWAYS: the siege-works -------------------------------------------

## the-salt-vault | The Salt-Vault
!existing
> east   the-cooperage

## the-breathing-hall | The Breathing-Hall
!existing
> east   the-lung-vent

## the-cooperage | The Cooperage
Where the garrison's barrels were made and mended: staves stacked in racks, hoops
rusted into rings of red lace, a bench with the drawknife still lying on it. The
tide comes into this room only as far as the sill, and has left a clean line to
prove it.
> west   the-salt-vault
> south  the-cable-walk

## the-cable-walk | The Cable-Walk
A long, narrow room where the tideway's ropes were laid, with hooks at one end and a
turning-wheel at the other, and between them sixty paces of rope stretched taut and
gone as hard as wood. Something has been gnawing at it, patiently, from the middle.
> north  the-cooperage
> east   the-brine-cellar

## the-brine-cellar | The Brine Cellar
Vats of brine are sunk into the floor, their lids long gone and the brine crusted
over white and thick as ice. Under the crust of the largest vat something is
floating. It has been pickled so well and for so long that it still has its face.
> west   the-cable-walk
> south  the-lung-vent

## the-lung-vent | The Lung-Vent
A shaft in the wall where the wing's air comes and goes. Stand in front of it and it
draws on you, slow and strong, pulling at your clothes and your breath; then it lets
go and breathes out cold and salt into your face. Whatever it breathes through is a
long way down.
> west   the-breathing-hall
> north  the-brine-cellar

# ---- EACH KING'S OWN ROOM ----------------------------------------------------

## the-marrow-seat | The Marrow Seat
Past the font the choir has built itself a throne: a high seat of fused long-bones
and ribs on a dais of skulls, turned back toward the singing. The cantors made it,
and they are still making it. The newest bones in it are white. Whoever wears the
crown sits here while the choir sings to him, and every time, he is wound a little
further into the seat.
> north  the-bone-font

## the-still-cradle | The Still Cradle
!existing
> down   the-gods-pool

## the-gods-pool | The God's Pool
Below the bottom there is one more room, and it is nearly all water: a round black
pool from wall to wall, with a rim of stone just wide enough to stand on. The water
rises and falls, slow as breathing, and every time it rises it comes a little
further over the rim. Something in it was a king once. It has been sitting in the
water so long that it no longer knows which of them is which.
> up     the-still-cradle
